# buzz_routes.env — non-secret route table for bin/deliver.sh.
#
# Three lines per route key, and nothing else belongs here:
#   ROUTE_<key>=<buzz-channel-uuid>    where it goes
#   ROUTE_<key>_kind=<9|45001>         what event kind it is published as
#   ROUTE_<key>_notify=<slug|none>     which agent it wakes
# No pubkeys, no credentials — a notify value is a slug that bin/buzz_agents.env resolves,
# and the signing identity is loaded only by the credential helper under the deny-listed
# ~/.config/buzz-agents/ tree.
#
# Channel UUIDs are stable across renames; update a value only when the destination
# channel UUID itself changes.
#
# An EMPTY value means "route not configured yet". deliver.sh then skips Buzz, still
# attempts Discord, and records a config_error receipt — it never fails the caller.
# The six channels were created 2026-08-07. Channel creation needs no owner key — any
# relay member can create, add members to, and delete a channel, and becomes its owner.
# The one step that genuinely requires the Mac is admitting a NEW identity to the relay,
# because the NIP-OA auth tag has to be signed by the owner. Verified 2026-08-07.

# The kind is a property of the DESTINATION, not of the producer: two producers writing
# the same channel must publish the same kind or half the traffic is invisible to the
# reader. Streams are chronological feeds (kind 9); forums are artifact threads whose
# root is kind 45001. Desktop's forum view queries kinds:[45001] exclusively, so a kind-9
# post into a forum channel is accepted by the relay, receipted `ok`, and never shown.
#
# 45003 (forum COMMENT) is deliberately not a legal value: buzz-cli requires --reply-to
# for it and no producer here replies to an existing thread. A missing _kind line means 9.
#
# The relay does not gate a content kind by channel type — forum kinds carry the same
# write scope as kind 9, and channel_type is read only on the channel-create path
# (buzz-relay ingest.rs:372-374 vs :2566-2640, upstream 02f640bc). So a 45001 into a
# stream-typed channel is accepted too; what changes is only how a client renders it.

# Every agent subscribes with `require_mention = true`, so a delivery carrying no `p` tag
# is dropped at dispatch by every one of them — it reaches the channel, receipts
# `delivered`, and wakes nobody. The notify slug is what makes a scheduled job reach an
# agent at all, and it belongs to the destination for the same reason the kind does: a
# route has one reader, however many producers write to it. `none` is the explicit way to
# say "post it, wake nobody", and is not a fault. The named agent must be a member of that
# channel — buzz-cli rejects the whole send otherwise, it does not drop the tag.

ROUTE_ops=62f321f3-bd6a-4b31-b19b-b8b49bed30f4
ROUTE_ops_kind=9
ROUTE_ops_notify=marcus
ROUTE_research=6ea596af-248f-46ee-b89d-8b13696083e4
ROUTE_research_kind=45001
ROUTE_research_notify=claudius
ROUTE_content=36dc03cb-5b24-45f6-b6d4-ac6de33c4621
ROUTE_content_kind=45001
ROUTE_content_notify=augustus
ROUTE_bd=97b5cf17-2d08-4ac4-9714-445ab8133471
ROUTE_bd_kind=45001
ROUTE_bd_notify=claudius
ROUTE_signals=a8e99852-278b-4ca9-8efd-51d33f336011
ROUTE_signals_kind=9
ROUTE_signals_notify=claudius
ROUTE_approvals=aceeddd2-f76c-490c-94dd-ee9d30701768
ROUTE_approvals_kind=45001
ROUTE_approvals_notify=marcus
